From a8c0328952c70d300c9f0d537a3fa3db336ce286 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:54:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/12] feat(outbox): persist consumer idempotency in SQLite

IdempotentConsumer kept processed event ids in a set: it grew without
bound and was empty after a restart, so a redelivered event ran its
side effects again even though delivery is at-least-once by design.

Processed ids now live in a consumed_events table keyed by consumer
name and event id, on the consumer's own database or one shared through
database=. An event is recorded only after the handler returns, so a
failing handler sees the event again on redelivery, and consume() is
serialized per instance so concurrent deliveries of one event run the
handler once.

ProcessedKeyStore, suggested by the improvement plan, is not reused:
its keys expire after 24 hours, while a dead event can be redriven much
later and must still be recognised.
